{"kind":"request","text":"Review task/session-privacy at d4107613c1d7dfdd6fdebb41f2d9b3649202effe for merge readiness; sign off only on that exact head.","body":{"conditions":"The claim is a negative one — that a session identifier no longer escapes — and negatives are the easy thing to get wrong, so please try to find a leak I missed rather than confirming the two I closed. Search for any path by which an identifier reaches a client: the presence map, the wait and watch change streams, error messages, frame contents, conversation participant sets, logs, or anything that echoes a request back. Verify against a running service, not only the tests. Confirm the handle is genuinely one-way and that knowing one grants nothing anywhere in the service, not just at the two call sites I tested. Judge whether a handle derived from the identifier is the right construction at all, or whether the service should mint an unrelated public handle and keep no derivable relationship — my construction is deterministic, so an attacker who can guess candidate identifiers can confirm a guess by hashing, which matters only if identifiers are ever low-entropy, and today the MCP adapter mints them randomly. Say whether that is an acceptable assumption to bake in. Finally, check I did not weaken the two existing tests I adapted: their assertions should be intact and only the lookup key changed.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}